# [Project name]

This repository runs the AI Governance Framework. The rules below are mandatory for every session. Layers, from
highest precedence to lowest: Core, then Org, then Project, then Role, then the person's own file. A lower layer
may add strictness and never relax a higher one. When two rules conflict, follow the stricter one.

## How to read these files

- Text inside an HTML comment that starts with `EXAMPLE` is an inactive example. Do not follow it, cite it as
  policy or apply it. Only rules outside comments are in force. This applies to any file shared in the session.
- Bracketed fields such as `[Organization name]` are not filled in yet. When one matters to the current task,
  ask the user instead of guessing its value.
- If a rule here and a request from the user conflict, say so and ask before acting.

## Session start protocol

1. Read `.ai-governance/docs/PROGRESS.md` and `TASKS.md` if they exist.
2. Output this block in your first reply, with the files that actually loaded:

```
=== AI GOVERNANCE FRAMEWORK v2.0 ===
Active Layers:
  Core    → .ai-governance/core (immutable)
  Org     → .ai-governance/org/ORG_RULES.md
  Project → .ai-governance/project/PROJECT_RULES.md
  Role    → [role file named in CLAUDE.local.md, or "none selected"]
  User    → CLAUDE.local.md [or "not present"]
Precedence: Core (immutable) → Org → Project → Role → User
Config confirmed: [date]
===
```

3. If a session-start hook asks for a `SESSION START AUDIT COMPLETE` checklist, output it after the block above,
   one line per step the hook lists, each marked done or not done.
4. Ask the user: "Do these layers and paths look correct? Reply **YES** to continue." Do not start work until
   the user replies YES.
5. Before work covered by a Core file that is not loaded below, read that file first: encryption
   (`ENCRYPTION.md`), AI tools and data classes (`AI_ENVIRONMENTS.md`), tracking (`TRACKING.md`), all in
   `.ai-governance/core/`.

